#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092012 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/id #uuid "a74be952-4dd4-5a7f-a8a4-de68977c5bb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5457" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(-5,-1), (1,4), (3,-5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by an angle of $90^\\circ$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,-5)$\n\n- $(4,-1)$\n\n- $(-5,-3)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by an angle of $90^\\circ$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of $270^\\circ.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S$ as columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-1,5), (4,-1), (-5,-3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092013 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/id #uuid "e945271e-2180-559b-9998-53ab9982043c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5458" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(2,-1), (-3,5),$ and $(4,-3).$  Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by an angle of $\\pi$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(-2,1)$\n\n- $(-4,3)$\n\n- $(-5,3)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by an angle of $\\pi$ radians is given by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S$ as columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-2,1), (3,-5), (-4,3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092061 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "7091b406-96d1-5663-9575-66f26c6a6267" :question/math-academy-id "e-7186" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/a43a1658a16151c0306702959c50e8d0288077983ad50a084b39703e206469c0.png)\n\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ shown above. Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $A,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/414cb72ff004fe4c5898a0e2c7b60b4b3651473e776388fad21efd8fc9ae6165.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $A\\mathbf{v},$ we need to rotate $\\mathbf{v}$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/b24d8d460b7833a49f2bd7183f0f4b5d22d94a7d3b1d3bb9eb51beff9eeb7ef6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f7b110b-04b2-5dff-9776-a58956cadb89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283664} {:answer/id #uuid "40dd50a4-b29a-5f50-bc4f-56a7c986dd26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283665} {:answer/id #uuid "0b5120bc-03c3-561a-9972-81c26fc7d824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283666} {:answer/id #uuid "ef45b474-23d4-5db1-b303-9eba3be7ca1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283667} {:answer/id #uuid "a282c8ad-012f-5852-9cf1-fb7fb0ba2903" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a282c8ad-012f-5852-9cf1-fb7fb0ba2903" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283668} :answer-field/id #uuid "52390b55-2534-5dcd-a08a-0142a01ed0c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0af2085-c5e5-5d4f-bf4d-73318bbad63b" :question/math-academy-id "q-160371" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(6,-1),(-4,1),(3,5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(-6,-1)$\n- $(4,-1)$\n- $(3,-5)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-6,1),(4,-1),(-3,-5).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "533fe1e0-736f-56d4-808f-c1d14b2743d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283685} {:answer/id #uuid "1dceeb35-0cb2-5a43-8e49-86d4583f28b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283686} {:answer/id #uuid "c7eb0977-8a2d-5218-b250-3fa7d83eb3ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283687} {:answer/id #uuid "d5c0cc42-8a0b-5c9c-ac44-596917ab7319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283688} {:answer/id #uuid "3d16dded-e01f-512b-b59e-d410e6956f17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dceeb35-0cb2-5a43-8e49-86d4583f28b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283686} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd86fb08-e9a6-50ff-89b2-48d975d8101e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37ce60a1-7ee2-5ebe-8858-c59ff464ba06" :question/math-academy-id "q-160643" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(0,1),(-1,0),(-1,-2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(0,-1)$\n- $(-2,1)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,0),(0,1),(-2,1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b8cce3-342c-5d43-9666-d1f16ea5ef6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283692} {:answer/id #uuid "3da4c708-2c9b-5200-b8c9-1c381670eedf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283693} {:answer/id #uuid "c6666819-cfa2-5225-956a-905d8b63b19f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283694} {:answer/id #uuid "9cbb4d98-4041-5882-8d32-a049dad37879" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283695} {:answer/id #uuid "0a85f801-e304-50a1-bbb5-53fc60815182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a85f801-e304-50a1-bbb5-53fc60815182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283696} :answer-field/id #uuid "2701afd1-8c74-50cf-8cb6-61b818707a0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f78fbb4-97dc-56c5-a843-630741ba1e9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-160645" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-3,1),(1,-4),(1,0).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(-1,3)$\n- $(4,1)$\n- $(0,-1)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{90}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,-3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,-4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{90}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\,-3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,-4 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-1,-3),(4,1),(0,1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "583eebc8-9b82-554d-b2de-5c335db960f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283713} {:answer/id #uuid "5ae11bac-66c8-5798-8fa5-a2f753aa3798" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283714} {:answer/id #uuid "ed32353d-285a-581f-84a7-665c32f26c9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283715} {:answer/id #uuid "14e4b63d-27b0-5e04-a172-a0cd24c58b38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283716} {:answer/id #uuid "341b1054-3b59-571d-b0bb-be4696fb5426" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "583eebc8-9b82-554d-b2de-5c335db960f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283713} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3733fa4-37ac-5b8e-8d55-f9b595ba5660" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4efc51d3-b393-57b6-8514-a14b7c1d9942" :question/math-academy-id "q-81819" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(4,4),(-3,-3),(-1,7).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(3,-3)$\n- $(-4,-4)$\n- $(1,-7)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-4,-4),(3,3),(1,-7).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27b5f433-bd4e-5f65-b834-ac07a468506c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/ca19bbcea3f01e99b0c606ad719686d84bca7b4dbfa1596f7b28e27fa494c44c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283720} {:answer/id #uuid "f2233722-a9fc-5e5b-8758-4ebf4e5d9785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/90dcf60e19c9031927df68a1d0be6b85c47910e6ec92e9052c292f667971d7f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283721} {:answer/id #uuid "6e76ce5f-f648-553e-a359-f4b73443423f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/c95c1f93ccc6c6c2ff7264ac5bca90680ff2c3c745d95acb0f81d288bf4e498a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283722} {:answer/id #uuid "734be4d4-9ce8-5ddc-912e-36e3b26d62b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/9dc6d9d844c382cc5a8499929dfe7d3a9ab8bf59fc5b73b2cd4dab44c51e135f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283723} {:answer/id #uuid "91cbe6e5-ab80-59af-a30c-e9bcef95a102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/c612d528e8fb432e4f63fdaae2649a4a41b0f3bed7751df5b5cc22074b57bb53.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2233722-a9fc-5e5b-8758-4ebf4e5d9785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/90dcf60e19c9031927df68a1d0be6b85c47910e6ec92e9052c292f667971d7f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283721} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a85a070-43e5-5b3f-b15a-8b65d3878416" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15275e29-2842-51f0-82c7-9a7b3b14ec04" :question/math-academy-id "q-81820" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/e6741c165a0f0f50bf932dd04c6d4c1157b4f70a8dc4c63efc5416b3883124bc.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/4e0e5c9cee037bded1f5739af35c3a8acfaa4d4e6f469b3e50afe69b96990e39.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/45f0ff53440247c533dd2b97ae03c8cd3e2e762b67832ce770c0bd5749e74f6e.png)"}]}
